Water pumping is one of the biggest needs in farming. Traditional pumps run on grid electricity or diesel, which means running costs and power availability are constant worries for farmers. A solar pump uses electricity from solar panels to run the pump during sunlight hours.
How does a solar pump work?
Solar panels → Controller → Pump → Water distribution
The pump can draw water from a borewell, pond, reservoir or other source, depending on the installation.
What are the benefits?
•	Lower dependence on grid electricity
•	Lower or no diesel cost
•	Daytime pumping from free solar energy
•	Works well in areas with weak or no grid supply
•	Can be combined with drip or sprinkler irrigation
Government support: PM-KUSUM
Under the PM-KUSUM scheme, the central and state governments provide financial assistance to farmers for standalone solar pumps and for solarising existing grid-connected pumps. The farmer pays only a part of the cost, as per the current scheme guidelines in West Bengal.
Subsidy shares and application windows change from time to time, so check the current status with the state nodal agency or with us before applying.
How do you choose pump capacity?
Do not choose a pump based only on horsepower. Consider:
•	Borewell depth and water level
•	Required flow rate and total head
•	Pipe length and diameter
•	Crop type and daily water requirement
•	Available sunlight at the site
A properly designed system avoids both under-sizing (not enough water) and over-sizing (wasted money).
