Meta description: Drip or sprinkler irrigation? Compare how each works, which crops suit each, and what to consider before choosing — plus subsidy information for West Bengal farmers.

Choosing the right irrigation system has a big impact on water use and crop yield. The two most common modern methods are drip irrigation and sprinkler irrigation.
What is drip irrigation?
Drip irrigation delivers water slowly, directly at the root zone of each plant. It is useful for:
•	Vegetables
•	Orchards and fruit crops
•	Flowers
•	Row crops
•	Nursery applications
What is sprinkler irrigation?
Sprinkler irrigation sprays water over the field like rainfall. It can suit:
•	Field crops
•	Vegetables
•	Nurseries
•	Lawns
•	Some horticulture crops
Quick comparison
Point	Drip	Sprinkler
How water is given	Directly at the roots	Sprayed over the field
Water saving	Highest	Good
Best for	Spaced crops, orchards, vegetables	Closely planted field crops
Fertiliser through water	Very effective (fertigation)	Possible

Which one should you choose?
There is no single answer. The choice depends on crop, soil type, farm layout, water availability, terrain, plant spacing, budget and the level of automation you want.
Subsidy for micro-irrigation
Drip and sprinkler systems are eligible for subsidy under PMKSY – Per Drop More Crop. Small and marginal farmers usually get a higher share. Check the current rates with your block agriculture office or ask us.
