There is no fixed cost for installing drip irrigation on one acre. The final price depends on the crop, plant spacing, water source, field shape, filtration, pipe network and automation level.
Factors that affect the cost
Crop spacing: an orchard and a vegetable farm need very different layouts and pipe quantities.
Water source: the distance to the water source affects the main pipeline and pump requirement.
Filtration: good filters keep emitters working properly for years.
Fertigation: fertiliser can be applied through the same system, which adds equipment but saves labour.
Automation: automatic controls increase the initial cost but give better control.
Subsidy can reduce your cost
Under PMKSY – Per Drop More Crop, farmers can get government subsidy on drip irrigation systems. The subsidy share depends on the farmer category and current state guidelines. Check with your block agriculture office or ask us to help with the paperwork.
Why not compare only the price per acre?
Two systems for the same area can have completely different specifications. Compare:
•	Pipe and lateral quality
•	Emitter quality
•	Filtration
•	Pump integration
•	Fertigation
•	Installation quality
•	Warranty, maintenance and spare parts
